::ALG1_U06_L02_Q01::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q01] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve y = x + 4 and x + y = 12.
{
~A. (2, 10)#This satisfies x + y = 12 but not y = x + 4.
~B. (10, 2)#This reverses the relationship.
=C. (4, 8)#Correct. Substitute to get x + x + 4 = 12, so x = 4 and y = 8.
~D. (6, 6)#This satisfies x + y = 12 but not y = x + 4.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q02::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q02] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 2x + y = 13 and x - y = 2.
{
~A. (4, 3)#This gives 2x + y = 11, not 13.
=B. (5, 3)#Correct. Add the equations to get 3x = 15, so x = 5 and y = 3.
~C. (3, 5)#This reverses the coordinates.
~D. (6, 1)#This satisfies x - y = 5, not 2.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q03::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q03] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which first step is best for y = 3x - 2 and x + y = 10?
{
=A. Substitute 3x - 2 for y in x + y = 10#Correct. The first equation already gives y.
~B. Add the equations immediately#The variables are not aligned to eliminate a variable.
~C. Divide both equations by x#Dividing by a variable is not a valid first step here.
~D. Set x = 0#Choosing one value does not solve the system.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q04::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q04] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 3x + y = 16 and x - y = 4.
{
~A. (3, 7)#This gives x - y = -4, not 4.
~B. (7, 3)#This gives 3x + y = 24, not 16.
~C. (4, 4)#This gives x - y = 0, not 4.
=D. (5, 1)#Correct. Add the equations to get 4x = 20, so x = 5 and y = 1.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q05::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q05] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve y = 2x + 1 and y = x + 6.
{
=A. (5, 11)#Correct. Set 2x + 1 = x + 6, so x = 5 and y = 11.
~B. (1, 3)#This satisfies y = 2x + 1 but not y = x + 6.
~C. (6, 5)#This reverses the expected relationship.
~D. (11, 5)#This reverses x and y.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q06::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q06] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 4x - y = 10 and x + y = 5.
{
~A. (2, 3)#This reverses the coordinates and does not satisfy both equations.
~B. (5, 0)#This satisfies x + y = 5 but not 4x - y = 10.
=C. (3, 2)#Correct. Add the equations to get 5x = 15, so x = 3 and y = 2.
~D. (1, 4)#This satisfies x + y = 5 but not 4x - y = 10.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q07::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q07] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which method is most efficient for 2x + y = 9 and 5x - y = 12?
{
~A. Substitution after solving both equations for y#That works but takes extra steps.
~B. Graphing by hand#Graphing may be less exact for algebraic quiz work.
~C. Multiplying both equations by -1#That does not create an immediate solution.
=D. Elimination by adding the equations#Correct. The y-terms are opposites and cancel immediately.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q08::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q08] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve y = -x + 7 and 2x + y = 11.
{
~A. (2, 5)#This gives 2x + y = 9, not 11.
=B. (4, 3)#Correct. Substitute to get 2x + (-x + 7) = 11, so x = 4 and y = 3.
~C. (5, 2)#This gives y = 2 but -x + 7 = 2, so it works in the first equation but not the second.
~D. (3, 4)#This reverses the coordinates.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q09::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q09] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 2x + 3y = 14 and 2x - y = 6.
{
~A. (6, 2)#This gives 2x - y = 10, not 6.
=B. (4, 2)#Correct. Subtract the equations to eliminate x, then solve for y.
~C. (2, 6)#This gives 2x + 3y = 22, not 18.
~D. (3, 4)#This gives 2x + 3y = 18 but 2x - y = 2.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q10::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q10] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
After solving a system, why should the ordered pair be checked in both equations?
{
~A. It changes the solution into slope-intercept form#Checking does not rewrite the equations.
~B. It removes the need to solve#Checking comes after solving.
~C. It makes the coefficients equal#Checking substitutes values; it does not change coefficients.
=D. It confirms both equations are true for the same x and y#Correct. A system solution must satisfy every equation.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q11::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q11] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve y = 4x and x + y = 15.
{
=A. (3, 12)#Correct. x + 4x = 15, so x = 3 and y = 12.
~B. (5, 10)#This satisfies x + y = 15 but not y = 4x.
~C. (10, 5)#This does not satisfy y = 4x.
~D. (12, 3)#This reverses x and y.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q12::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q12] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 5x + y = 19 and 2x - y = 2.
{
~A. (2, 9)#This gives 2x - y = -5, not 2.
~B. (9, 2)#This gives 5x + y = 47, not 19.
=C. (3, 4)#Correct. Add the equations to get 7x = 21, so x = 3 and y = 4.
~D. (4, 3)#This reverses the coordinates and does not satisfy both equations.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q13::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q13] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which first step helps solve 3x + 2y = 18 and 3x - y = 9 by elimination?
{
=A. Subtract the second equation from the first to eliminate x#Correct. The 3x terms cancel when the equations are subtracted.
~B. Add the equations to eliminate x#Adding gives 6x, so x is not eliminated.
~C. Divide by y#Dividing by a variable is not a valid elimination step.
~D. Replace x with 0#Choosing a value does not solve the system.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q14::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q14] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve x + y = 10 and x - y = 2.
{
~A. (4, 6)#This gives x - y = -2, not 2.
=B. (6, 4)#Correct. Add the equations to get 2x = 12, so x = 6 and y = 4.
~C. (5, 5)#This gives x - y = 0, not 2.
~D. (8, 2)#This gives x + y = 10 but x - y = 6.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q15::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q15] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve y = x - 3 and y = -2x + 6.
{
~A. (0, -3)#This is only on y = x - 3.
~B. (6, -6)#This is only on y = -2x + 6.
~C. (0, 3)#This reverses the solution idea.
=D. (3, 0)#Correct. Set x - 3 = -2x + 6, so x = 3 and y = 0.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q16::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q16] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which statement describes substitution correctly?
{
~A. Add the equations so one variable cancels#That describes elimination.
~B. Graph both lines and read the crossing point#That describes graphing.
=C. Replace a variable with an equivalent expression#Correct. Substitution uses one equation to replace a variable in the other.
~D. Guess values until both equations work#That is not an efficient algebraic method.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q17::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q17] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 2x + y = 8 and y = x - 1.
{
=A. (3, 2)#Correct. Substitute x - 1 for y: 2x + x - 1 = 8, so x = 3 and y = 2.
~B. (2, 3)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (1, 0)#This satisfies y = x - 1 but not 2x + y = 8.
~D. (4, 1)#This does not satisfy y = x - 1.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q18::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q18] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 3x + y = 17 and x + y = 9.
{
~A. (2, 7)#This gives 3x + y = 13, not 17.
=B. (4, 5)#Correct. Subtract the equations to get 2x = 8, so x = 4 and y = 5.
~C. (7, 2)#This gives 3x + y = 23, not 17.
~D. (5, 4)#This gives x + y = 9 but 3x + y = 19.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q19::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q19] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which ordered pair satisfies 4x + y = 21 and x + y = 9?
{
~A. (5, 4)#This reverses x and y and does not satisfy both equations.
~B. (3, 6)#This satisfies x + y = 9 but not 4x + y = 21.
=C. (4, 5)#Correct. Subtract the equations to get 3x = 12, then y = 5.
~D. (6, 3)#This satisfies x + y = 9 but not 4x + y = 21.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q20::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q20] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve y = 5x - 7 and y = 2x + 2.
{
~A. (2, 3)#This works in y = 5x - 7 but not y = 2x + 2.
~B. (8, 3)#This reverses x and y.
~C. (1, -2)#This does not satisfy both equations.
=D. (3, 8)#Correct. Set 5x - 7 = 2x + 2, so x = 3 and y = 8.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q21::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q21] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which method is most direct for x = 2y + 1 and x + y = 13?
{
=A. Substitution#Correct. Substitute 2y + 1 for x in x + y = 13.
~B. Elimination without rewriting#The variables are not aligned to cancel immediately.
~C. Graphing only#Graphing can work but is not the most direct algebraic method.
~D. Dividing both equations by 13#This does not isolate a variable or eliminate one.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q22::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q22] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve 2x - y = 7 and 2x + y = 13.
{
~A. (4, 5)#This gives 2x - y = 3, not 7.
~B. (3, 5)#This reverses the coordinates.
~C. (6, 1)#This gives 2x + y = 13 but 2x - y = 11.
=D. (5, 3)#Correct. Add the equations to get 4x = 20, so x = 5 and y = 3.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q23::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q23] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
After substitution, a student gets 4x + 8 = 20. What is x?
{
~A. x = 7#This divides 28 by 4 after adding instead of subtracting.
=B. x = 3#Correct. Subtract 8 to get 4x = 12, then divide by 4.
~C. x = 5#This divides 20 by 4 without subtracting 8.
~D. x = 12#This stops after subtracting 8.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q24::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q24] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Solve x + 2y = 14 and x - y = 2.
{
=A. (6, 4)#Correct. Subtract the second equation from the first to get 3y = 12, so y = 4 and x = 6.
~B. (2, 6)#This gives x - y = -4, not 2.
~C. (6, 2)#This gives x + 2y = 10, not 14.
~D. (4, 5)#This gives x - y = -1, not 2.
}

::ALG1_U06_L02_Q25::[Question ID: ALG1_U06_L02_Q25] [MLA Standard: MLA.A1.AR.5 support]
Which answer format is correct for a system solution?
{
~A. x only#This leaves out the y-coordinate.
~B. y only#This leaves out the x-coordinate.
=C. (x, y)#Correct. A system solution is an ordered pair.
~D. slope only#Slope is not the ordered-pair solution.
}
